The statement is **false** in general. Here is a counterexample.

**Counterexample.** Consider the measure space $(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}), m)$ where $m$ is Lebesgue measure (an infinite measure space). Define
$$f_n = \chi_{[n,\,n+1]}, \qquad f \equiv 0.$$

**Claim 1: $f_n \to f$ almost everywhere (in fact, everywhere).**

Fix any $x \in \mathbb{R}$. Choose an integer $N > x$ (e.g., $N = \lfloor x \rfloor + 1$). Then for every $n \geq N$, we have $x < n$, so $x \notin [n, n+1]$, hence $f_n(x) = 0 = f(x)$. Thus $f_n(x) \to f(x)$ for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$, and in particular $f_n \to f$ almost everywhere.

**Claim 2: $f_n \not\to f$ in measure.**

Recall that $f_n \to f$ in measure requires, for every $\varepsilon > 0$,
$$m\big(\{|f_n - f| \geq \varepsilon\}\big) \to 0 \quad \text{as } n \to \infty.$$

Take $\varepsilon = \tfrac{1}{2}$. Since $f \equiv 0$ and $f_n$ takes only the values $0$ and $1$,
$$\{|f_n - f| \geq \tfrac{1}{2}\} = \{f_n \geq \tfrac{1}{2}\} = [n, n+1].$$

Therefore
$$m\big(\{|f_n - f| \geq \tfrac{1}{2}\}\big) = m([n,n+1]) = 1 \quad \text{for all } n.$$

This does **not** converge to $0$, so $f_n \not\to f$ in measure.

**Conclusion.** We have exhibited a sequence $(f_n)$ with $f_n \to f$ almost everywhere but $f_n \not\to f$ in measure. Hence the statement

> "If $f_n \to f$ almost everywhere, then $f_n \to f$ in measure."

is **false** in general. (It becomes true under the additional hypothesis that the measure space is finite, by Egorov's theorem.)

### PROOF COMPLETE
